# The Fortress Is Crumbling!
Tamil title: கோட்டை சரிகிறது!

The Hindu Religious Endowments Protection Amendment Bill is now under examination by a select committee. Chosen by the Madras Legislative Assembly, that committee is collecting the views of people from various sections of the public concerning the bill.

Orthodoxy has not failed to send representatives at this moment to place its views before the committee. Many born into the priestly tradition have seized the occasion and come forward forcefully to urge that the bill be abandoned. Many are educated intellectuals, acquainted with the world and fully aware of the country's condition. Nevertheless, habitual communal feeling makes them fear that the rights and advantages traditionally enjoyed by their groups must not be destroyed by this bill, and drives them desperately to prevent its implementation.

They oppose it by saying that making it law would harm Hinduism itself; that the government would incur the great sin of interfering in religious affairs; that the distinction and greatness of heads of monasteries would be impaired; and that the world would be forced to languish in misery because orthodox Agamic rites could no longer be performed.

Heads of monasteries have opened their iron chests and set money rolling out to prevent the bill. Many have been hired to argue for them and sent before the select committee.

The public had wondered whether a bill introduced by Omandur Ramasamy Reddiar's ministry would be allowed to slip away by Kumaraswami Raja's ministry. When orthodox representatives placed their views before the select committee, one also had to wonder whether they would render the bill lifeless. But Comrade T. S. S. Rajan, the minister who introduced it in the legislature, said at a reception in Ooty:

“Despite opposition here and there, the necessity of reforming temples and monasteries has arisen. It is the government's duty to do so. Opposition will not make the government retreat.”

This statement gives room to believe that the bill may succeed.

The Dravidar Kazhagam does not regard the bill as a complete and final reform. It rejoices only because the measure forms one forward step along the path toward its ideal.

Through the bill, the government seeks merely to assume administrative responsibility for temples, monasteries and charitable rest houses. It does not attempt to change, reform or remove the ancient customs prevailing within them. Present rulers may regard it as a finished reform, but the Dravidar Kazhagam sees it only as partial reform. It is pleased that the government has come at least this far, and extends support to that extent.

For the Dravidar Kazhagam, the intended reform of temples, monasteries and rest houses does not stop here; it extends much further. The government may imagine that this bill places a full stop after reform. The Kazhagam, believing that much more remains to be done, places only a comma. It works to support the present measure only on the understanding that it can and must grow further.

Opponents argue that the government intends to interfere in religion and must not do so. Supporters explain that corruption has become abundant in the administration of temple and monastic properties, and that the government therefore has a duty to eliminate corruption and put administration in order.

Seeking to conceal the bill's fundamental purpose, educated orthodox representatives understand how to bewilder ordinary people and draw them to their side, so they cry that religion is in danger. They say religion's chief purpose is to make people renounce the desires for land, wealth and women that cause rebirth, help them cross the great ocean of birth, and immerse them in supreme bliss. They also say religion arose to explain the relationship between humanity and God and bring people clarity.

Every religion explains in its distinctive fashion the road to God and holds different doctrines. This shows a connection between ideas and religion. Religion should not concern itself with worldly property; property has no connection with religion. Governments are established precisely to concern themselves with property. Viewed in this manner, it becomes easy to determine whether government has interfered in religion or religion has interfered in government.

Has the Madras government interfered in doctrines belonging to religion? No. It has not insisted that God must be worshipped in a certain fashion, that Hindus must bear particular marks, read only certain religious books, or perform rites only in specified ways.

Had it insisted that the deity to be worshipped was Allah, Jehovah, Brahma or Hanuman; that the guides to worship must be the Prophet, Jesus, the Nayanmars, the Alvars, Buddha or Mahavira; or that the scripture to be accepted must be the Quran, Bible, Saiva Siddhanta, Vaishnavism or Vedanta, one could say it was interfering in religion. The Madras government insists on none of these.

After all this time, it merely proposes to take administrative responsibility for property that it ought to govern but that has become trapped and ruined in the hands of Hindu religion. In truth, the government would possess the right even to take all religious property, because religion cannot own property and should not desire it. Yet the Madras government has not dared to go even that far; it wishes only to assume responsibility for administration.

Turning the pages of history shows that religion interfered in government and appropriated property for itself. At one time gods sat as stone forms beneath trees, in hollows, caves and beside ponds. They possessed no property, and religion, which protected them, bore no duty of managing it.

Only under the autocratic rule of later Pallava, Chalukya, Vijayanagara, Chera, Chola and Pandya kings did temples, monasteries and rest houses acquire property. Mahendravarman Pallava, Narasimhavarman Pallava, Rajaraja Chola, Rajendra Chola, Kulothunga Chola, Sundara Pandya, Kulasekara Alvar, Cheraman Perumal Nayanar, Krishnadevaraya and others held all governing power. Believing nobody could question them, they poured tax revenue and other money paid by the public upon the gods each preferred.

A Vaishnavite built Vishnu temples; a Saivite built Siva temples; some built both. They used government money to erect towers, granted endowments to temples, made conches and silver vehicles, adorned images with gold and gems, conducted festivals, created many displays of splendour and assigned properties to support them all.

Likewise, great landlords and wealthy people who amassed fortunes by plundering the poor granted endowments to temples, monasteries and rest houses. Thus religions that preached renunciation of desire for land and gold enchanted kings and made government property their own. Religion also spent ages making the structure of government grant it privileges. History therefore shows that religion's excessive interference in government has far outweighed government interference in religion.

Does the government interfere in religion today? No. Does it therefore refuse to care for religions? No again. It wishes to embrace and protect them out of concern for their welfare. Yet religion still maintains its domination over government. Even the small influence once exercised by government over religion has disappeared entirely, while religion remains unwilling to relax even slightly the influence it established over government.

Is the present government, then, a secular government proceeding by rationalist principles? It is not that either. It calls itself secular, embraces every religion, and refuses to touch the doctrines of any.

Vaidyanatha Iyers and Varadacharis demand to know what right the government has to interfere with religious property. Did religion accumulate its property by raining gold from the sky through its own power? History says no. Who assigned property to religion? Was it not autocratic kings? Government property was handed to religion while the people slept. Now the people have awakened and seek to re-establish government authority over property once granted to religion. What is wrong in that?

If Varadacharis and Vaidyanatha Iyers tremble at this bill, what does that reveal? Only that the interests of their community must not be affected in any way and must be protected.

As Vaidyanatha Iyer himself previously stated in the Madras Assembly, one-third of the Madras Presidency's property is locked in temples and monasteries. A third of the country's wealth has become a plaything in the hands of wastrels. Vaidyanatha Iyer has arrived to argue that the “divine sports” of devotees must proceed without the slightest hindrance, while Annamalais and Sahajanandas provide accompaniment beside him.

According to figures Omandur Ramasamy Reddiar gave the Assembly, this country has 27,000 temples that maintain accounts of income and expenditure, 25,000 charitable rest houses that feed and sustain idlers, and 3,000 monasteries. Of the monasteries, 164 are large institutions. Their heads eat until swollen, apply sandalwood to their bellies to aid digestion, receive the white fly-whisk, spend their leisure caressing the flower-like feet of beautiful women, and devote their minds eternally to discovering the connection between this world and the next.

These institutions alone receive more than ten crore rupees each year. That entire sum is spent upon groups of idlers who eat and grow fat, without benefiting the working public in the slightest. Has entrusting so much property to religion created any means of raising the country's general condition?

God, said to be pure and beyond desire and aversion, needs no property because everything is supposedly his creation. He desires nothing because he supposedly possesses the power to create anything. He asks humanity for nothing because he supposedly needs nothing. Perhaps for these reasons “God” pays no attention to the theft and fraud committed in relation to property left in his name. Should not the government come forward to oversee property God has forgotten to watch? Why should this truth escape hardened orthodox believers? Perhaps devotional intoxication has overwhelmed them to that extent.

Omandur Ramasamy Reddiar explained in the Assembly the frauds committed in eighty-eight religious endowments. How many escaped the government's eye? How many were noticed but slipped away? How many continue secretly? Even the detected accounts show frauds ranging from 200 to 200,000 rupees in those eighty-eight institutions. The gods had merely watched until then; some order became possible only after government intervention.

If opponents argue that a government considered representative of the people must not administer temple and monastery property, they either desire more fraud and plunder under the administration of private, idle and autocratic monastery heads, gurus, priests and devotees, or unknowingly drive administration toward that condition. The rational public must consider whether such intrigue should be allowed.

Several years ago, when the Self-Respect Movement said that fraud occurred in religious property and that the government should take it over and spend it on good works benefiting the public, religious advocates and Congress members opposed the proposal. In some places they even attacked Self-Respecters. Today we are pleased to see Congress members employ that same method.

Hereditary orthodoxy has begun resisting in order to prevent the Hindu Religious Endowments Protection Amendment Bill from becoming law and taking effect. Rationalist artillery is attacking and demolishing the orthodox fortress from behind, while orthodox defenders try to resist at the front with spears called Veda and Agama.

The battle may last a little longer, but destruction will embrace them from behind without their knowledge. That much is certain. The roads connected to the gateway of the orthodox fortress are being closed one by one. This Religious Endowments Protection Amendment Bill is one such effort.

The rationalist movement welcomes and praises the Madras government to that extent for coming forward to perform this task. With every blast of rationalist artillery, it becomes plainly visible that the orthodox fortress is crumbling.

Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 12 June 1949.

---NOTES---
The scans directly establish the printed heading as `39. கோட்டை சரிகிறது!` and the publication date as `12.6.49`; both were corrected in the canonical OCR and organized Tamil sources before building this translation.

Some personal names and institutional labels remain OCR-uncertain, particularly the paired references to Vaidyanatha Iyer and Varadachari and the later names Annamalai and Sahajananda. They are transliterated conservatively and should be checked against the original issue for formal indexing.

The article's descriptions of religious officers and institutions preserve the author's polemical register. They are translated as historical source text rather than presented as independent factual findings.
